.\"
.\" SEM_CLOSE(3) - Substrate Pthreads Library
.\"
.TH SEM_CLOSE 3 "June 2026" "Substrate OS" "Library Functions"
.SH NAME
sem_close \- close a named semaphore
.SH LIBRARY
POSIX threads library
.RI ( libpthread ", " \-lpthread )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <semaphore.h>
.PP
.BI "int sem_close(sem_t *" sem ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR sem_close ()
closes the named semaphore referred to by
.I sem
and releases the resources the process allocated for it in
.BR sem_open (3).
The underlying kernel object (see
.BR ksem_close (2))
is not destroyed until the semaphore has also been removed with
.BR sem_unlink (3)
and every process that opened it has closed it.
.PP
.I sem
must have been returned by
.BR sem_open (3).
Passing an unnamed
.RB ( sem_init (3))
semaphore is an error; use
.BR sem_destroy (3)
for those.  Using
.I sem
after
.BR sem_close ()
returns is undefined.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns 0 on success.  On failure it returns \-1 and sets
.I errno
to indicate the error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I sem
is not a valid named semaphore descriptor.
.SH EXAMPLE
.RS 4
.EX
sem_t *s = sem_open("/example", O_CREAT | O_RDWR, 0666, 1);
if (s == SEM_FAILED) { perror("sem_open"); return 1; }
/* ... use the semaphore ... */
sem_close(s);
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sem_open (3),
.BR sem_unlink (3),
.BR sem_destroy (3),
.BR ksem_close (2)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2017.
